1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,387
In the south of Costa Rica, among the banana fields, there are stone balls.

2
00:00:05,387 --> 00:00:06,532
Hundreds of them.

3
00:00:06,532 --> 00:00:08,015
Some fit in your hand.

4
00:00:08,015 --> 00:00:12,866
The biggest is taller than a person and weighs about twenty-four tonnes.

5
00:00:13,333 --> 00:00:19,077
They are so round that, for decades, people have said no human hand could have made them.

6
00:00:19,077 --> 00:00:22,433
Perfect to within two millimetres, one author wrote.

7
00:00:22,899 --> 00:00:25,115
And so the theories rolled in.

8
00:00:25,115 --> 00:00:28,659
Guidance markers for the navigators of Atlantis.

9
00:00:28,659 --> 00:00:32,277
Relics of a lost world twelve thousand years old.

10
00:00:32,277 --> 00:00:35,600
Even orbs dropped by visitors from the stars.

11
00:00:36,066 --> 00:00:39,564
Today we do not argue about the stones from a distance.

12
00:00:39,564 --> 00:00:47,833
We follow one of them through its whole life, from the moment it was a boulder in a river, to the day it became a national symbol.

13
00:00:48,299 --> 00:00:53,912
At every stage of that life, we check what the evidence says, and what the theory says.

14
00:00:53,912 --> 00:00:57,266
At the end, we open the ledger and give the verdict.

15
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:03,535
Chapter one: the boulder.

16
00:01:03,535 --> 00:01:10,166
Our sphere begins as a rock in the mountains of the Talamanca range, in southern Costa Rica.

17
00:01:10,633 --> 00:01:21,500
Over thousands of years, rivers like the Térraba tumble pieces of hard volcanic rock downstream, knocking off the corners, rounding them into boulders.

18
00:01:21,966 --> 00:01:33,022
Most spheres are made of gabbro or a similar rock, granodiorite: hard, coarse-grained, igneous stone that formed deep underground from cooled magma.

19
00:01:33,022 --> 00:01:38,700
A dozen or so are limestone made of shells, and another dozen are sandstone.

20
00:01:39,166 --> 00:01:52,099
The finished spheres are found down on the Diquís Delta, the flat floodplain where the Térraba and Sierpe rivers meet the Pacific, and on a small island offshore, Isla del Caño.

21
00:01:52,566 --> 00:01:58,338
So the raw material came from the mountains, and the river did the first rough work.

22
00:01:58,338 --> 00:02:02,667
Some claim the balls themselves are natural, polished by water.

23
00:02:02,667 --> 00:02:03,560
They are not.

24
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:08,233
Natural concretions exist elsewhere, but these are carved monoliths.

25
00:02:08,699 --> 00:02:09,746
A boulder, then.

26
00:02:09,746 --> 00:02:12,036
Hard, heavy, and already round-ish.

27
00:02:12,036 --> 00:02:13,933
Now someone has to choose it.

28
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:16,017
Chapter two: the making.

29
00:02:16,017 --> 00:02:26,533
No workshop has ever been found with a half-made giant in progress, so we reconstruct the method from the stones themselves, and from how this rock behaves.

30
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,242
This kind of rock has a weakness.

31
00:02:29,242 --> 00:02:36,309
Heat it with hot coals, then dash it with cold water, and it peels away in curved layers, like an onion.

32
00:02:36,309 --> 00:02:39,300
That lets a crew rough out a sphere quickly.

33
00:02:39,766 --> 00:02:42,228
Then comes the slow part: pecking.

34
00:02:42,228 --> 00:02:47,948
Thousands upon thousands of blows with hammerstones made of the same hard rock.

35
00:02:47,948 --> 00:02:50,699
Each strike crushes away a few grains.

36
00:02:51,166 --> 00:02:52,797
And that is not a guess.

37
00:02:52,797 --> 00:02:57,282
Some spheres still carry the small pits left by hammerstone blows.

38
00:02:57,282 --> 00:03:00,000
The tool marks are written on the stone.

39
00:03:00,466 --> 00:03:06,042
Finally the surface is ground with sand, water and flat stones, and polished.

40
00:03:06,042 --> 00:03:12,488
The same people made polished stone axes, intricately carved grinding stones and statues.

41
00:03:12,488 --> 00:03:14,733
They knew this craft very well.

42
00:03:15,199 --> 00:03:17,063
The alternative versions?

43
00:03:17,063 --> 00:03:17,957
Laser beams.

44
00:03:17,957 --> 00:03:19,075
Alien machines.

45
00:03:19,075 --> 00:03:24,366
Or a secret liquid that softened rock so it could be moulded like clay.

46
00:03:24,833 --> 00:03:33,170
The softening potion appears in local legends, and a researcher once suggested plant acids could dissolve limestone.

47
00:03:33,170 --> 00:03:38,633
But most spheres are gabbro, an igneous rock that acid simply does not melt.

48
00:03:39,099 --> 00:03:41,767
And here is a clue the theorists skip.

49
00:03:41,767 --> 00:03:43,452
The finish varies a lot.

50
00:03:43,452 --> 00:03:45,980
Some spheres are beautifully smooth.

51
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:47,173
Others are lumpy.

52
00:03:47,173 --> 00:03:51,666
That is what you expect from human workshops, not from machines.

53
00:03:52,133 --> 00:03:54,133
Fire, water, stone and time.

54
00:03:54,133 --> 00:03:55,990
That is the whole toolkit.

55
00:03:55,990 --> 00:03:58,133
But how perfect is the result?

56
00:03:58,599 --> 00:04:00,479
Chapter three: the measure.

57
00:04:00,479 --> 00:04:10,433
Almost every claim of perfection traces back to one man: Harvard archaeologist Samuel Lothrop, who studied the spheres in nineteen forty-eight.

58
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:18,493
His wife Eleanor later recalled his equipment: a tape measure, and a fishing line with a lead sinker on the end.

59
00:04:18,493 --> 00:04:19,307
A plumb bob.

60
00:04:19,307 --> 00:04:22,833
That is what the famous precision was measured with.

61
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:27,769
Lothrop himself called his methods not completely satisfactory.

62
00:04:27,769 --> 00:04:36,566
Big balls were often deeply buried, so his team dug around only the top half, and assumed the hidden half was just as round.

63
00:04:37,033 --> 00:04:44,764
For the best balls, they wrapped the tape around in several directions and then calculated the diameter mathematically.

64
00:04:44,764 --> 00:04:48,533
Those calculations were printed to several decimal places.

65
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,753
Later writers read those decimals as measured precision.

66
00:04:52,753 --> 00:05:01,533
That is how 'perfect to within two millimetres' was born, along with claims of spheres up to thirty tonnes and three metres across.

67
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,278
But a calculated average is not a measurement.

68
00:05:05,278 --> 00:05:09,839
Lothrop found the poorer balls varied by up to five centimetres.

69
00:05:09,839 --> 00:05:13,688
Their surfaces have bumps larger than two millimetres.

70
00:05:13,688 --> 00:05:16,966
Nobody has ever measured one to that accuracy.

71
00:05:17,433 --> 00:05:23,734
The biggest known sphere is about two point six metres across and about twenty-four tonnes.

72
00:05:23,734 --> 00:05:24,565
Astonishing.

73
00:05:24,565 --> 00:05:29,066
But not three metres, not thirty tonnes, and not machine-perfect.

74
00:05:29,533 --> 00:05:38,161
So the honest version is this: the best spheres are remarkably round, as round as skilled hands and patient eyes could make them.

75
00:05:38,161 --> 00:05:39,899
That is impressive enough.

76
00:05:40,366 --> 00:05:42,154
Chapter four: the village.

77
00:05:42,154 --> 00:05:44,697
Our finished sphere now needs a home.

78
00:05:44,697 --> 00:05:49,166
And the archaeology tells us where it went: into living villages.

79
00:05:49,633 --> 00:06:01,233
The people of the Diquís Delta lived in chiefdoms, ruled by chiefs, in round houses built on mounds with foundations of river cobbles, raised above the floods.

80
00:06:01,699 --> 00:06:07,104
Spheres are found with their pottery, their stone pavements and their burials.

81
00:06:07,104 --> 00:06:15,766
Lothrop dug under one ball and found a broken figurine head of a known local pottery style, made after about AD one thousand.

82
00:06:16,233 --> 00:06:18,238
So the dates come from layers.

83
00:06:18,238 --> 00:06:26,261
Spheres were being made from roughly AD six hundred, most of them after AD one thousand, and before the Spanish arrived.

84
00:06:26,261 --> 00:06:28,266
Not twelve thousand years ago.

85
00:06:28,733 --> 00:06:38,499
Excavations by Francisco Corrales of the National Museum found two spheres set on either side of the entrance ramp of a house platform.

86
00:06:38,499 --> 00:06:42,333
Like status markers at the door of someone important.

87
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:49,673
At the site called Finca Six, some spheres still sit in their original lines, roughly east to west.

88
00:06:49,673 --> 00:06:57,033
Lothrop mapped straight lines, curves and triangles, and one group of four that pointed to magnetic north.

89
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,715
This is where Atlantis enters.

90
00:06:59,715 --> 00:07:12,266
In a nineteen ninety-eight book, Ivar Zapp and George Erikson argued the lines were navigation pointers, aimed at Easter Island and Stonehenge, left by ancient seafarers.

91
00:07:12,733 --> 00:07:17,558
The problem: the alignments were measured over a few metres with a tape.

92
00:07:17,558 --> 00:07:21,779
Extend a tiny error across an ocean and you can point anywhere.

93
00:07:21,779 --> 00:07:25,666
And most spheres sit far from the sea, useless to sailors.

94
00:07:26,133 --> 00:07:30,533
What remains open is what the lines meant to their makers.

95
00:07:30,533 --> 00:07:34,478
Sunrise on certain days, constellations, or stories.

96
00:07:34,478 --> 00:07:38,500
Archaeologists say plainly: none of it is proven yet.

97
00:07:38,966 --> 00:07:40,784
Chapter five: the silence.

98
00:07:40,784 --> 00:07:46,098
Around the time of the Spanish conquest, the sphere-making world fell apart.

99
00:07:46,098 --> 00:07:47,566
The villages emptied.

100
00:07:48,033 --> 00:07:52,437
Spanish explorers reached this coast in the sixteenth century.

101
00:07:52,437 --> 00:07:55,350
They left no report of giant stone balls.

102
00:07:55,350 --> 00:07:59,400
The people who made them had already gone, or soon would.

103
00:07:59,866 --> 00:08:00,166
Why?

104
00:08:00,166 --> 00:08:01,289
We do not know.

105
00:08:01,289 --> 00:08:05,559
Catastrophic floods could have ruined harvests for years.

106
00:08:05,559 --> 00:08:11,177
War is possible too; local stone statues show figures holding trophy heads.

107
00:08:11,177 --> 00:08:13,500
Then came conquest and disease.

108
00:08:13,966 --> 00:08:17,170
And then the river did something unexpected.

109
00:08:17,170 --> 00:08:21,392
Flood after flood laid fine silt over the abandoned sites.

110
00:08:21,392 --> 00:08:25,033
Some spheres ended up a metre or more underground.

111
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:27,094
That burial was a gift.

112
00:08:27,094 --> 00:08:36,799
UNESCO credits those thick layers of sediment with saving the spheres from the looting that emptied most archaeological sites in Costa Rica.

113
00:08:37,266 --> 00:08:39,421
Memory survived in other ways.

114
00:08:39,421 --> 00:08:54,000
In Bribri tradition, shared by the Cabécar, the spheres are Tara's cannonballs: the thunder god shot them with a giant blowpipe at the Serkes, the gods of wind and hurricane, to drive them from the land.

115
00:08:54,466 --> 00:08:57,491
That is a living story, not a lab report.

116
00:08:57,491 --> 00:09:04,500
But notice whose story it is: Indigenous Costa Ricans, not visitors from Atlantis or the stars.

117
00:09:04,966 --> 00:09:06,838
Chapter six: the bulldozer.

118
00:09:06,838 --> 00:09:14,046
In the late nineteen thirties, the United Fruit Company cleared the delta jungle for banana plantations.

119
00:09:14,046 --> 00:09:16,333
The machines hit something round.

120
00:09:16,799 --> 00:09:19,451
Bulldozers shoved the spheres aside.

121
00:09:19,451 --> 00:09:24,533
Then a rumour spread among workers: the balls had gold hidden inside.

122
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,717
So they drilled holes and blew them open with dynamite.

123
00:09:28,717 --> 00:09:29,934
There was no gold.

124
00:09:29,934 --> 00:09:31,354
Only shattered stone.

125
00:09:31,354 --> 00:09:35,477
Several spheres were destroyed before authorities stepped in.

126
00:09:35,477 --> 00:09:39,533
Some were later glued back together for the National Museum.

127
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:48,873
Others rode the banana railway out of the delta and became lawn ornaments, outside banks, homes and government buildings.

128
00:09:48,873 --> 00:09:51,733
One even ended up in a garden in Miami.

129
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:53,884
Science arrived quickly.

130
00:09:53,884 --> 00:10:03,433
Doris Stone, daughter of a United Fruit executive, published the first study in nineteen forty-three, in the journal American Antiquity.

131
00:10:03,899 --> 00:10:11,166
Then came Lothrop's Harvard fieldwork, published in nineteen sixty-three, with maps, pottery and photographs.

132
00:10:11,166 --> 00:10:17,233
In the nineteen fifties he could still count about fifty spheres where they had been found.

133
00:10:17,233 --> 00:10:19,700
Today only a handful remain in place.

134
00:10:20,166 --> 00:10:22,047
Pop culture did the rest.

135
00:10:22,047 --> 00:10:27,161
The giant boulder that chases Indiana Jones is often linked to them.

136
00:10:27,161 --> 00:10:34,833
In nineteen seventy-one, Erich von Däniken's ancient astronaut books pulled them into the alien story.

137
00:10:35,299 --> 00:10:36,819
Here is the real twist.

138
00:10:36,819 --> 00:10:39,595
The mystery was not created by the makers.

139
00:10:39,595 --> 00:10:46,533
It was created by us, when we moved the spheres and destroyed the context that could have explained them.

140
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,049
Chapter seven: the homecoming.

141
00:10:49,049 --> 00:10:52,192
Costa Rica has been bringing its spheres home.

142
00:10:52,192 --> 00:10:58,000
Those still in the delta were protected as national treasures, and export was banned.

143
00:10:58,466 --> 00:11:03,444
In twenty fourteen, that sphere from the Miami garden came back by boat.

144
00:11:03,444 --> 00:11:09,182
It weighed about one and a half tonnes and had spent seventy-four years in Florida.

145
00:11:09,182 --> 00:11:11,533
Its owner returned it voluntarily.

146
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:27,133
Two months later, in June, UNESCO inscribed four sites as World Heritage: Finca Six, Batambal, El Silencio and Grijalba Two, the Precolumbian Chiefdom Settlements with Stone Spheres of the Diquís.

147
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:32,469
The largest sphere, at El Silencio, had been cracked by field fires.

148
00:11:32,469 --> 00:11:39,200
In twenty nineteen the National Museum, working with Mexican conservators, began restoring it.

149
00:11:39,666 --> 00:11:44,116
A month after UNESCO, the spheres were declared a national symbol.

150
00:11:44,116 --> 00:11:48,566
They stand at the Legislative Assembly and in the National Museum.

151
00:11:49,033 --> 00:12:00,033
And the people most likely descended from the makers are still here: the Boruca, the Térraba, the Ngäbe, Chibchan-speaking nations of southern Costa Rica.

152
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:09,330
Archaeologist John Hoopes suggests the spheres show people taking the river's power to shape stone, and making it their own.

153
00:12:09,330 --> 00:12:13,033
A boulder shaped by nature, finished by human hands.

154
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:15,518
Our sphere's life is complete.

155
00:12:15,518 --> 00:12:17,133
Time to open the ledger.

156
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:21,476
Shaped by fire, hammerstones and grinding: evidence.

157
00:12:21,476 --> 00:12:25,503
Found with local pottery, mounds and houses: evidence.

158
00:12:25,503 --> 00:12:29,752
Made roughly AD six hundred to fifteen hundred: evidence.

159
00:12:29,752 --> 00:12:33,033
Markers of status at chiefs' houses: likely.

160
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:36,221
Perfect to two millimetres: debunked.

161
00:12:36,221 --> 00:12:39,677
Atlantis navigators or alien orbs: no evidence.

162
00:12:39,677 --> 00:12:42,766
And what the alignments meant: still open.

163
00:12:43,233 --> 00:12:44,171
The verdict.

164
00:12:44,171 --> 00:12:55,431
The stone spheres of Costa Rica were made by the Diquís chiefdoms, Indigenous people of the delta, with fire, stone tools and enormous patience.

165
00:12:55,431 --> 00:12:56,213
No lasers.

166
00:12:56,213 --> 00:12:57,151
No Atlantis.

167
00:12:57,151 --> 00:12:57,933
No aliens.

168
00:12:58,399 --> 00:13:04,130
What stays open is their meaning: why the lines, why the sizes, why spheres at all.

169
00:13:04,130 --> 00:13:09,033
That answer is buried with lost context, and perhaps still in the silt.

170
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:13,553
And the real wonder is not that they are too perfect for humans.

171
00:13:13,553 --> 00:13:22,799
It is that humans, with river stones in their hands, made something this beautiful, and that it nearly vanished in a single century of bulldozers.

172
00:13:23,266 --> 00:13:25,094
That is the life of a stone.

173
00:13:25,094 --> 00:13:27,249
Which chapter surprised you most?

174
00:13:27,249 --> 00:13:32,733
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
